                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "In my opinion The Rare Nine is a mediocre 2D zombie game. There are plenty of better games out there for the same price tag.\n\n[h1]Positive:[/h1]\n- It’s nice that 9 characters are included. However, 6 of the 9 must first be unlocked. I would have liked to try playing the different characters, but I simply do not enjoy playing The Rare Nine enough to try to unlock everything.\n- Thankfully you get plenty of item drops. You will need them.\n- I thought it was funny that memes are hanging on the walls.\n\n[h1]Negative:[/h1]\n- No clear objective. There is no explanation in the game, you just spawn in a room, try to open the correct door, kill zombies, and proceed to the next (almost identical room) by opening random doors. I managed the reach the Leprechaun boss but my attacks did negligible damage, and his movement and attack speeds were much faster than mine, so I died after using all my items. I don’t know if I’m trying to survive as long as possible or how I should beat the game, but honestly, I’m not that interested to find out.\n- The zombie hitboxes are unclear to me (perhaps because their arms are fully extended?). Attacks look like they should have hit often don't. This is especially frustrating when playing a melee character. The attack cool downs are quite long, so a missed attack will often lead to zombie bites. \n- Character balancing is very strange. For example, the character with the chainsaw has about half the attack range of a punching character. As far as I could tell, both the punch and chainsaw does the same amount of damage, so why would anyone want to play Mr. Chainsaw? Furthermore, the second character is clearly overpowered compared to the others. Why? His attacks are basically bottle throws that send bottles from one side of the room to the other, penetrates enemies, and kills them all in one hit. It’s like playing the game on easy mode (still got destroyed by the boss though).\n- No enemy health bar only a “HIT” notification. Most enemies die in one hit, so it’s not a big issue. But sometimes they take more hits (at least from what I could tell). Some kind of health indicator would be nice.\n- The game feels luck- rather than skill-based. No indication where zombies spawn (or how many will). You can only hope to encounter as few zombies as possible, that they spawn in a manageable fashion, and that you get enough item drops that, so you can use them liberally. There is no real way to avoid damage in this game, so you better spam that med kit and adrenaline shot.\n\n[h1]Conclusion:[/h1]\nVery little interests me about this game. The graphics and audio are average, there’s no clear objective, and the gameplay isn’t fun. As it stands, I have no intention of playing The Rare Nine again.\n\n[table]\n[tr]\n[th]If you enjoyed this review please follow me on my [url=http://store.steampowered.com/curator/26827738-%27b-Reviews/?appid=241930]curator page[/url] and [url=http://steamcommunity.com/groups/breviews]group[/url]. Your time and support is greatly appreciated![/th]\n[/tr]\n[/table]\n\n[code]This review was submitted for [url=http://steamcommunity.com/groups/gaming_until_end ]Gaming Until End[/url], through the generous contribution of [url=http://steamcommunity.com/id/HellCowON]HellCow.[/url] [/code]",
